      "has_proof": true,
      "statement_event": 51,
      "proof_event": 52,
      "proof_chapter": "fermats-last-theorem-for-regular-primes",
      "proof_leanok": true,
      "text": "Let $p \\geq 3$ be an prime number, $\\zeta_p$ a $p$-th root of unity and $ \\a \\in \\ZZ[\\zeta_p]$. Let $x$ and $y$ be integers such that $x+y\\zeta_p^i=u \\a^p$ with $u \\in \\ZZ[\\zeta_p]^\\times$ and $\\a \\in \\ZZ[\\zeta_p]$. Then there is an integer $k$ such that \\[x+y\\zeta_p^i-\\zeta_p^{2k}x-\\zeta_p^{2k-i}y \\equiv 0 \\pmod p.\\]"
    },
