      "proof_chapter": "fermats-last-theorem-for-regular-primes",
      "proof_leanok": true,
      "text": "Let $p \\geq 3$ be an prime number, $\\zeta_p$ a $p$-th root of unity and $K=\\QQ(\\zeta_p)$. Assume that we have $x,y,z \\in \\ZZ$ with $\\gcd(xyz,p)=1$ and such that \\[x^p+y^p=z^p.\\] This is the so called \"case I\". To prove Fermat's last theorem, we may assume that: \\begin{itemize} \\item $p \\geq 5$; \\item $x,y,z$ are pairwise coprime; \\item $x \\not \\equiv y \\mod p$. \\end{itemize}"
    },
